<LessonLayout title="Our Vocation to Beatitude" cic={lessonRef} scripture={lessonScripture}>

<section class="content">

<h2>Big Question</h2>

<p class="big-question">What would truly make you happy?</p>

<p>
A young man approached a wise old man and asked: &laquo;Master, what is the secret of happiness?&raquo; The old man asked him to follow him to a river. There, he took the young man's head and plunged it into the water. The young man began to struggle, desperate for air. When the old man released him, the young man gasped deeply. The old man asked: &laquo;What did you want most in that moment?&raquo; &laquo;To breathe!&raquo; the young man replied. The old man smiled: &laquo;That is how intense your desire for God must be. When you desire God with the same urgency with which you desired air, then you will find him. And in him, true happiness.&raquo; We all seek happiness. It is the deepest desire of the human heart. But where to find it? God himself has placed this desire in us, and he alone can fully satisfy it.
</p>
</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>The Desire for Happiness</h2>

<p>
St. Augustine expressed it with a phrase that has resonated for centuries: &laquo;You have made us for yourself, O Lord, and our heart is restless until it rests in you.&raquo; The desire for happiness is inscribed in the heart of every person. There is no one who does not want to be happy. This desire is good, because it has been placed there by God himself (CCC 1716-1718).
</p>

<p>
The problem is that we often seek happiness in the wrong places: in pleasure, in money, in power, in recognition. These things can give momentary happiness, but they do not satisfy the heart &mdash; like drinking salt water: it quenches thirst for a moment but makes the thirst return even stronger. Full happiness is found only in God. The beatitudes are the path that Jesus shows us to attain that happiness (CCC 1718-1719).
</p>

<div class="depth-box depth-box--semilla">
<h4 class="depth-box__title">Activity</h4>
<p>Write a list of five things you think would make you happy. Now ask yourself: do any of these things last forever? What happens if I lose them? Is there anything that no loss can take from me?</p>
</div>
</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>What are the Beatitudes?</h2>

<p>
The beatitudes are the heart of Jesus' preaching. They stand at the center of the Sermon on the Mount (Mt 5:3-12) and are like a &laquo;portrait of Christ&raquo;: they describe what he is and what his disciples are called to be. They are &laquo;the way of happiness&raquo; that God promises to his children (CCC 1716-1717).
</p>

<p>
The eight beatitudes are:
</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Blessed are the poor in spirit</strong>, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven (Mt 5:3).</li>
  <li><strong>Blessed are the meek</strong>, for they will inherit the land (Mt 5:4).</li>
  <li><strong>Blessed are those who mourn</strong>, for they will be comforted (Mt 5:5).</li>
  <li><strong>Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for righteousness</strong>, for they will be satisfied (Mt 5:6).</li>
  <li><strong>Blessed are the merciful</strong>, for they will obtain mercy (Mt 5:7).</li>
  <li><strong>Blessed are the pure in heart</strong>, for they will see God (Mt 5:8).</li>
  <li><strong>Blessed are the peacemakers</strong>, for they will be called children of God (Mt 5:9).</li>
  <li><strong>Blessed are those who are persecuted for righteousness' sake</strong>, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven (Mt 5:10).</li>
</ol>

<p>
Each beatitude is a promise. They do not describe an ideal situation, but the reality of those who live in the Kingdom of God, even in the midst of difficulties. They are a paradox: they call &laquo;blessed&raquo; the poor, those who suffer, the persecuted &mdash; not because suffering is good, but because the Kingdom that is coming transforms all reality (CCC 1717).
</p>

<div class="depth-box depth-box--brotes">
<h4 class="depth-box__title">Connection</h4>
<p>The world calls the rich, the powerful, the successful blessed. Jesus calls the poor, the meek, the merciful blessed. Who is right? Have you ever tried to live according to the beatitudes? What was the experience like?</p>
</div>
</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>The Beatitudes: Path to Holiness</h2>

<p>
The beatitudes are not just an ethical ideal, but a path to holiness. They are the response to the universal call to holiness, which the Second Vatican Council strongly recalled: all Christians, in whatever state of life, are called to the perfection of love (CCC 1716-1724).
</p>

<p>
Each beatitude describes an attitude of the heart that makes us like Christ:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Poor in spirit:</strong> recognizing that everything we receive comes from God, living with humility and detachment.</li>
  <li><strong>Meek:</strong> not responding to violence with violence, trusting in God's justice.</li>
  <li><strong>Those who mourn:</strong> having a heart that grieves over sin and the suffering of the world.</li>
  <li><strong>Hunger and thirst for righteousness:</strong> ardently desiring that God's will be done in the world.</li>
  <li><strong>Merciful:</strong> forgiving as we have been forgiven, loving without conditions.</li>
  <li><strong>Pure in heart:</strong> having a heart without duplicity, transparent before God and others.</li>
  <li><strong>Peacemakers:</strong> building peace wherever we are, being instruments of reconciliation.</li>
  <li><strong>Persecuted:</strong> remaining faithful to Christ even when it costs something.</li>
</ul>

<div class="depth-box depth-box--raiz">
<h4 class="depth-box__title">Going Deeper</h4>
<p>The beatitudes are not &laquo;laws&raquo; in the moralistic sense. They are the manifestation of the Kingdom of God that is already present and will be fully fulfilled at the end of time. Living the beatitudes is anticipating heaven on earth. It is living already as citizens of the Kingdom, even while we are in the world (CCC 1720-1721). Which beatitude do you find most difficult to live? And which one do you find most attractive?</p>
</div>
</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Eternal Beatitude</h2>

<p>
Ultimately, the beatitudes orient us toward our final destiny: eternal beatitude, the vision of God face to face. The Catechism reminds us that this is the true happiness for which we were created: &laquo;God put us in the world to know him, serve him, and love him, and so to reach paradise&raquo; (CCC 1720).
</p>

<p>
What is eternal beatitude? &laquo;What no eye has seen, nor ear heard, nor the human heart conceived, what God has prepared for those who love him&raquo; (1 Cor 2:9). It will be participation in the very life of God, &laquo;perfect life with the Most Holy Trinity&raquo; (CCC 1722-1724). It will be the rest of the heart that Augustine longed for. It will be happiness without shadows, without limits, without end.
</p>

<p>
But this destiny is not only future: it begins now. Grace is the beginning of glory. Every act of love, every beatitude lived, brings us closer to that full happiness. Eternal beatitude is not a prize we earn, but a gift we receive and begin to experience from now (CCC 1725-1729).
</p>

<div class="depth-box depth-box--arbol">
<h4 class="depth-box__title">Going Deeper</h4>
<p>The Catechism relates beatitude to the &laquo;beatific vision&raquo;: seeing God face to face (1 Jn 3:2; 1 Cor 13:12). St. Thomas Aquinas teaches that perfect happiness consists in the vision of the divine essence. It is not an emotional ecstasy, but an intellectual and loving knowledge of God that transforms us and fills us with joy. This vision is possible because God elevates us with the light of glory (lumen gloriae). Have you ever stopped to think that your destiny is to see God face to face?</p>
</div>
</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Biblical Connection: The Sermon on the Mount</h2>

<blockquote>
<p>&laquo;Seeing the crowds, he went up on the mountain, and when he sat down, his disciples came to him. And he opened his mouth and taught them, saying: Blessed are the poor in spirit, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven...&raquo;</p>
<p class="bible-passage__ref">&mdash; Matthew 5:1-3</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
The Sermon on the Mount is the &laquo;Magna Carta&raquo; of the Kingdom of God. Jesus goes up the mountain as Moses went up Sinai, but he does not give a law of stone, but the law of the heart. The beatitudes are the constitution of the Kingdom: they define who are the citizens of heaven and how they live. They are not an ethical code, but a portrait of Jesus and a promise of what we will be if we follow him.
</p>
</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>For Reflection</h2>

<ol>
  <li>Where have you sought happiness so far? In what things have you placed your hope?</li>
  <li>Which of the beatitudes do you find most attractive? And which do you find most difficult to live?</li>
  <li>The world says: happy are the successful, those who win, those who are famous. How does that contrast with the beatitudes?</li>
  <li>Have you known someone who truly lives according to the beatitudes? What impression did they make on you?</li>
  <li>Which beatitude would you like to live more intensely this week?</li>
</ol>
</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>

<div class="callout">
<p><strong>Key Takeaway:</strong> The happiness everyone seeks is not found in having, power, or pleasure, but in living as children of God, following the path of the beatitudes.</p>
</div>
</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Cultural Resources</h2>

<p>
<strong>St. Francis of Assisi</strong> &mdash; No one embodies the beatitudes better than St. Francis. As a rich young man, he left everything for Christ. He lived in poverty, meekness, and joy. His life was a living beatitude: &laquo;Perfect joy,&raquo; as he called it, consists not in success but in being faithful to Christ even in the midst of suffering.
</p>

<p>
<strong>&laquo;The Beatitude of the Kingdom&raquo; by Fra Angelico</strong> &mdash; His paintings of heaven, especially in the Convent of San Marco in Florence, show the blessed enjoying the vision of God. The soft colors, serene expressions, golden light: Fra Angelico gives us a foretaste of what it means to &laquo;see God.&raquo;
</p>

<p>
<strong>St. Th&eacute;r&egrave;se of Lisieux and the &laquo;Little Way&raquo;</strong> &mdash; St. Th&eacute;r&egrave;se discovered a path to holiness accessible to everyone: spiritual childhood. It is the beatitude of the &laquo;pure in heart&raquo; lived with childlike simplicity. Her &laquo;little way&raquo; is a concrete way of living the beatitudes in ordinary life.
</p>
</section>

